{"id":"workloads-controllers-replicaset","corpus":"k8s","title":"ReplicaSet","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/replicaset/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"ReplicaSet"},{"kind":"p","text":"A ReplicaSet's purpose is to maintain a stable set of replica Pods running at any given time. Usually, you define a Deployment and let that Deployment manage ReplicaSets automatically."},{"kind":"p","text":"A ReplicaSet's purpose is to maintain a stable set of replica Pods running at any given time. As such, it is often used to guarantee the availability of a specified number of identical Pods."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How a ReplicaSet works"},{"kind":"p","text":"A ReplicaSet is defined with fields, including a selector that specifies how to identify Pods it can acquire, a number of replicas indicating how many Pods it should be maintaining, and a pod template specifying the data of new Pods it should create to meet the number of replicas criteria. A ReplicaSet then fulfills its purpose by creating and deleting Pods as needed to reach the desired number. When a ReplicaSet needs to create new Pods, it uses its Pod template."},{"kind":"p","text":"A ReplicaSet is linked to its Pods via the Pods' metadata.ownerReferences field, which specifies what resource the current object is owned by. All Pods acquired by a ReplicaSet have their owning ReplicaSet's identifying information within their ownerReferences field. It's through this link that the ReplicaSet knows of the state of the Pods it is maintaining and plans accordingly."},{"kind":"p","text":"A ReplicaSet identifies new Pods to acquire by using its selector. If there is a Pod that has no OwnerReference or the OwnerReference is not a Controller and it matches a ReplicaSet's selector, it will be immediately acquired by said ReplicaSet."},{"kind":"h2","text":"When to use a ReplicaSet"},{"kind":"p","text":"A ReplicaSet ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at any given time. However, a Deployment is a higher-level concept that manages ReplicaSets and provides declarative updates to Pods along with a lot of other useful features. Therefore, we recommend using Deployments instead of directly using ReplicaSets, unless you require custom update orchestration or don't require updates at all."},{"kind":"p","text":"This actually means that you may never need to manipulate ReplicaSet objects: use a Deployment instead, and define your application in the spec section."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Example"},{"kind":"p","text":"Saving this manifest into frontend.yaml and submitting it to a Kubernetes cluster will create the defined ReplicaSet and the Pods that it manages."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can then get the current ReplicaSets deployed:"},{"kind":"p","text":"And see the frontend one you created:"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can also check on the state of the ReplicaSet:"},{"kind":"p","text":"And you will see output similar to:"},{"kind":"p","text":"And lastly you can check for the Pods brought up:"},{"kind":"p","text":"You should see Pod information similar to:"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can also verify that the owner reference of these pods is set to the frontend ReplicaSet. To do this, get the yaml of one of the Pods running:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The output will look similar to this, with the frontend ReplicaSet's info set in the metadata's ownerReferences field:"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Non-Template Pod acquisitions"},{"kind":"p","text":"While you can create bare Pods with no problems, it is strongly recommended to make sure that the bare Pods do not have labels which match the selector of one of your ReplicaSets. The reason for this is because a ReplicaSet is not limited to owning Pods specified by its template-- it can acquire other Pods in the manner specified in the previous sections."},{"kind":"p","text":"Take the previous frontend ReplicaSet example, and the Pods specified in the following manifest:"},{"kind":"p","text":"As those Pods do not have a Controller (or any object) as their owner reference and match the selector of the frontend ReplicaSet, they will immediately be acquired by it."},{"kind":"p","text":"Suppose you create the Pods after the frontend ReplicaSet has been deployed and has set up its initial Pod replicas to fulfill its replica count requirement:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The new Pods will be acquired by the ReplicaSet, and then immediately terminated as the ReplicaSet would be over its desired count."},{"kind":"p","text":"Fetching the Pods:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The output shows that the new Pods are either already terminated, or in the process of being terminated:"},{"kind":"p","text":"If you create the Pods first:"},{"kind":"p","text":"And then create the ReplicaSet however:"},{"kind":"p","text":"You shall see that the ReplicaSet has acquired the Pods and has only created new ones according to its spec until the number of its new Pods and the original matches its desired count. As fetching the Pods:"},{"kind":"p","text":"Will reveal in its output:"},{"kind":"p","text":"In this manner, a ReplicaSet can own a non-homogeneous set of Pods"}],"lemmas":{"purposes":["purpose","purposes"],"purposed":["purposed","purpose"],"maintains":["maintains","maintain"],"maintained":["maintained","maintain"],"stables":["stable","stables"],"stabled":["stabled","stable"],"sets":["set","sets"],"setting":["setting","set"],"pods":["pod","pods"],"running":["running","run"],"runnings":["running","runnings"],"runs":["run","runs"],"ran":["ran","run"],"given":["given","give"],"givens":["given","givens"],"gives":["give","gives"],"giving":["giving","give"],"gave":["gave","give"],"times":["time","times"],"timed":["timed","time"],"timing":["timing","time"],"usuallies":["usuallies","usually"],"defines":["defines","define"],"defining":["defining","define"],"lets":["let","lets"],"letting